# Rank 1894 / CVE-2025-61594: URI::Generic retains a URI's password
# across a change of authority, so merging a base URI that carries
# credentials with a network-path reference that carries none
# re-attaches the base's password to the reference's host (credential
# leak to a different origin, CWE-200; branch CVE-2025-61594-3-4,
# merge d3116ca66a, parent 3213f4a fully vulnerable). The root is
# set_userinfo in lib/uri/generic.rb: `@password = password if
# password` writes the field only when the incoming value is present,
# so a nil userinfo leaves the previous password in place. merge then
# calls `base.set_userinfo(rel.userinfo)` -- nil for a reference with
# only a host -- and swaps the host underneath it, producing
# http://:secret@b.test/ from http://user:secret@a.test/ merged with
# //b.test/. The same retention makes `uri.user = 'other'` carry the
# old password to the new user and `uri.host =`/`uri.port =` carry the
# old userinfo to the new authority. The fixed revision makes the write
# unconditional, clears the password in set_user and the userinfo in
# host=/port=, and gives merge an atomic set_authority(user, password,
# host, port) fed by a new authority reader, so no component of the
# base's authority survives a reference that does not name it. No pool
# correction: the commit is the merge of the change branch, its diff
# reads in full against the parent, and the parent carries the
# vulnerable form.
#
# ATTENTION, re-measured end to end at the current definitions state.
# Three --cache off runs per revision (timeout 180, --max-ram 6GB)
# report 25 findings on the parent and 25 on the fixed revision, 7 at
# or above high on both, and the fingerprint union per side differs in
# exactly one entry: the same find_proxy source-sink pair
# (generic.rb:1491 -> :1554 at the parent, :1504 -> :1567 at the fix),
# displaced by the 13 lines the fix adds above it. `-flags only`
# reports 2 flags on each side, byte-identical. Every finding is the
# generic library.param-source advisory and sink noise on unrelated
# files (rfc2396_parser regex tables, common.rb URI.extract,
# find_proxy's env read) -- zero on set_userinfo, set_user, host=,
# port= or merge at either revision.
#
# No rule asks the question this weakness needs. The nearest shipped
# family is VYQL-SEC-052 (code.CredentialHeadersForwardedAcross
# Redirect), whose bindings key on a Location read, a next-request
# header store and a redirect budget -- facts a URI composer does not
# spell, because it composes components rather than following a 3xx --
# so that family cannot be widened to cover it. The URI-internal-state
# family of ranks 1582, 1583 and 1606 records the same shape of
# blocker: the defect sits in a library's private state handling, and
# a binding on the library's own internals detects this library rather
# than the weakness.
#
# The diagnosis commands, run fresh:
#
# - `vyql match` labels no source, sink or control in any of the five
#   methods that carry the weakness. set_userinfo's `user` and
#   `password` parameters do carry the generic library.param-source
#   ExternalEntryInput label, and `vyql trace -from ExternalEntryInput`
#   counts 67 sources dead-ending with one reachable pair in the file
#   -- the unrelated find_proxy env read. The password is
#   library-internal state and never reaches a bound sink.
# - `vyql resolve` reports 336 resolved/recognized and 305 unresolved
#   call sites; `self.set_userinfo(userinfo)` at generic.rb:198 lowers
#   to the dropped-self `?.set_userinfo` form and stays unresolved.
# - `vyql explain` on the minimal pair below reports no findings, so
#   nothing is being suppressed by an `unless`: no rule matches the
#   shape at all.
#
# The two candidate bindings are measured, not assumed:
#
# 1. Root site (set_userinfo). A probe binding on the function's
#    assign token matches -- `assign:@password=password` is emitted --
#    but identically at both revisions, down to the same fingerprint:
#    the `if password` modifier contributes no structured token
#    (rbStructuredContextTokens has cases for assignment/binary/pair/
#    call/element_reference/string/regex and none for a modifier
#    guard), so the conditional and unconditional writes are
#    indistinguishable through node.context. The only route that sees
#    the modifier is the raw body text (`node.token contains
#    "if password"`, re-measured 1 -> 0 on the minimal pair below), and
#    it is comment-silenceable: a commented-out copy of the guarded
#    write inside the fixed form reports the finding. Keyed on a
#    private setter's `@password = v if v` spelling it also names this
#    library's incident rather than a weakness class -- the uri gem's
#    documentation (userinfo=, user=, host=) never describes
#    nil-retention, and a generic form would need a regex over the
#    field and guard identifiers, which presence predicates reject.
#
# 2. Exploit site (merge). The vulnerable merge calls
#    base.set_userinfo(rel.userinfo), base.set_host(rel.host) and
#    base.set_port(rel.port); the patched merge calls
#    base.set_authority(*authority) fed by rel.authority. Every token
#    that separates the two revisions is a name the fixed revision
#    itself introduced (set_authority, authority), the discriminator
#    shape rejected at rank 1394 -- and the pre-existing names the
#    vulnerable form uses are uri's own protected setters, so a
#    binding keying on them detects this gem rather than the weakness
#    class. The call sequence itself is not a weakness signal either
#    way: RFC 2396 resolution legitimately sets the base's userinfo,
#    host and port from a reference that carries them, so the caller
#    cannot discriminate a fix centralised in the callee.
#
# The retention alone is not the vulnerability, and that is what makes
# the root site unshippable rather than merely hard. Across the Ruby
# trees available for a collateral sweep the guarded
# credential-shaped field write occurs once -- this file -- while the
# unqualified guarded instance-variable write `@x = v if v` occurs 29
# times in 15 trees, every one a benign optional-set setter
# (`@chunked = app_chunking if app_chunking`, `@authenticate = blk if
# blk`, `@display_name = new_dn if new_dn`). What turns the retention
# into a leak is the authority change in another method, and an
# instance variable written in one method is not read in another -- the
# interprocedural field summary the Ruby frontend does not build, the
# same gap that blocks the Rails-concern shape at rank 1735. A rule
# judging "a component write clears the sibling components of its
# group" needs that relation plus a notion of component grouping;
# neither exists, and minting the vocabulary before the facts would
# leave it inert.
#
# No definition change shipped; no ENGINE-GAPS.md entry: the modifier
# token absence is real but secondary -- with a `modifier:` token the
# root binding would still fail the genericity test above, so this is
# vocabulary that does not exist, not a construct the engine mishandles
# on an otherwise shippable route.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-precision-limit, no rule can see
# credential state surviving an authority swap; both forms pinned as
# reject below

test "rank1894: the conditional password write and the host-swapping merge retain the base URI's credential"
  lang ruby
  file lib/uri/generic.rb
  reject VYQL-SEC-052
  code
  ```
  module URI
    class Generic
      def initialize(userinfo, host, port)
        @user = nil
        @password = nil
        @host = nil
        @port = nil
        self.set_userinfo(userinfo)
        self.set_host(host)
        self.set_port(port)
      end

      def split_userinfo(ui)
        return nil, nil unless ui
        user, password = ui.split(':', 2)
        [user, password]
      end
      private :split_userinfo

      def set_userinfo(user, password = nil)
        unless password
          user, password = split_userinfo(user)
        end
        @user     = user
        @password = password if password

        [@user, @password]
      end
      protected :set_userinfo

      def set_user(v)
        set_userinfo(v, @password)
        v
      end
      protected :set_user

      def set_host(v)
        @host = v
      end
      protected :set_host

      def set_port(v)
        @port = v
      end
      protected :set_port

      def userinfo
        [@user, @password] if @user || @password
      end

      def host
        @host
      end

      def port
        @port
      end

      def merge(rel)
        base = self.dup

        authority = rel.userinfo || rel.host || rel.port

        if authority
          base.set_userinfo(rel.userinfo)
          base.set_host(rel.host)
          base.set_port(rel.port)
        end

        base
      end
    end
  end

  # URI.parse('http://user:secret@a.test/').merge('//b.test/')
  #   # => "http://:secret@b.test/" -- the base's password, re-attached
  #   to the reference's host by a nil userinfo that cannot clear it
  ```

test "rank1894: the fixed revision writes the password unconditionally and swaps the authority atomically"
  lang ruby
  file lib/uri/generic.rb
  reject VYQL-SEC-052
  code
  ```
  module URI
    class Generic
      def initialize(userinfo, host, port)
        @user = nil
        @password = nil
        @host = nil
        @port = nil
        self.set_host(host)
        self.set_port(port)
        self.set_userinfo(userinfo)
      end

      def split_userinfo(ui)
        return nil, nil unless ui
        user, password = ui.split(':', 2)
        [user, password]
      end
      private :split_userinfo

      def set_userinfo(user, password = nil)
        unless password
          user, password = split_userinfo(user)
        end
        @user     = user
        @password = password

        [@user, @password]
      end
      protected :set_userinfo

      def set_user(v)
        set_userinfo(v, nil)
        v
      end
      protected :set_user

      def set_host(v)
        @host = v
      end
      protected :set_host

      def set_port(v)
        @port = v
      end
      protected :set_port

      def userinfo
        [@user, @password] if @user || @password
      end

      def host=(v)
        check_host(v)
        set_host(v)
        set_userinfo(nil)
        v
      end

      def port=(v)
        check_port(v)
        set_port(v)
        set_userinfo(nil)
        v
      end

      def authority
        return @user, @password, @host, @port if @user || @password || @host || @port
      end

      protected def set_authority(user, password, host, port = nil)
        @user, @password, @host, @port = user, password, host, port || self.default_port
      end

      def merge(rel)
        base = self.dup

        authority = rel.authority

        if authority
          base.set_authority(*authority)
          base.set_path(rel.path)
        end

        base
      end
    end
  end

  # URI.parse('http://user:secret@a.test/').merge('//b.test/')
  #   # => "http://b.test/" -- no component of the base's authority
  #   survives a reference that does not name it
  ```
